The '''Bolodun and Kuvari Islands''' are an island country in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]]. They comprise approximately 15 islands with a combined area of about 526 square kilometres and an estimated population of 115,593. The capital and largest city is [[Ominira]], on the island of [[Bolodun]]. The country is a multi-party socialist presidential republic, divided into fourteen districts that correspond to the major islands, and is a member of the [[Adonian Community]].

The islands were originally inhabited by the [[Kuvari people]], a maritime trading people of the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea. They were reached by the expedition of [[Guled Badweyn]] in 1520 — a few months before his arrival at [[Guajicutea]] — and incorporated into the [[Badweyn Islands]] colony. Developed as a sugar-producing plantation colony managed by the [[Lacashe]] province, the islands passed to the [[Capuyaquiran League]] in 1755. A brutal civil conflict following emancipation divided the islands between a Sudanese Kaftian administration on [[Bolodun]] and a Kuvari administration on the neighbouring island, a division that shaped relations with the rest of the League for two centuries. The country joined the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]] in 1832 and declared independence in 1926 after the [[Socialist Party of Bolodun]] won a legislative majority.

== Geography ==

The Bolodun and Kuvari Islands form a dispersed island group at the outer edge of the Capuyaquiran Sea. The country consists of fifteen islands with a total land area of approximately 526 square kilometres, giving an average population density of about 220 inhabitants per square kilometre. The islands cluster in two groups: [[Bolodun]] and [[Tumala]] in the north, and the smaller central and southern Kuvari chain.

