== History ==

=== Pre-colonial period ===

The islands were settled by the Kuvari people, a maritime trading people of the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea. The Kuvari islands were trade contacts of Guajicutea rather than tributaries; they exchanged goods with the wider island network while maintaining their own local leadership and seafaring traditions. Their position at the outer edge of the Capuyaquiran Sea brought them into contact with the trade routes linking the region to the wider Adonian world.

=== Discovery and the Badweyn Islands colony ===

In 1520, the expedition of the Burawi explorer [[Guled Badweyn]] passed west through the Capuyaquiran archipelago and reached the Kuvari islands, several months before Badweyn arrived at Guajicutea in December of that year. The islands were subsequently incorporated into the [[Badweyn Islands]] colony, the Burawi-administered group of former Capuyaquiran territories.

The colony was managed by the [[Lacashe]] province of [[New Burawa]]. Its islands were developed as sugar plantations worked by enslaved labour, and Bolodun emerged as the principal island of the group. In 1695, Bolodun was separated administratively from the rest of the colony and given its own governor, who administered all of the islands formerly occupied by the Kuvari people. The islands became a major producer of sugar within the Burawi colonial economy.

=== Abolition, emancipation, and the Kuvari insurgency ===

When the [[Capuyaquiran League]] was formed in 1755, slavery continued to be practiced on the islands. It was abolished with the [[Abolition of Slavery Act]] in [[Burawa]] in 1789, which also committed Burawa to cease trading with any state that traded in slaves. Facing the loss of its major export market, the League banned slavery the same year.

The [[Emancipation of Slaves Act (Capuyaquiran League)|Emancipation of Slaves Act]] ordered the relocation of emancipated slaves who did not sign contracts to work on existing farms to the island of Bolodun, which was granted autonomous control under the leader [[Iacob Kheme]]. The influx of freed Sudanese Kaftians onto Bolodun, where the Kuvari population had lived under an oppressive social hierarchy, produced an era of civil unrest. The League provided military support against the resulting Kuvari insurgency, fought from 1796 to 1801. The conflict culminated in the killing of large numbers of Kuvari on Bolodun and the expulsion of the survivors to the other islands.

The islands thereafter had a troubled relationship with the rest of the League for the next two centuries. Two administrations emerged in the aftermath of the war: one on Bolodun, run by Sudanese Kaftians, and one on [[Tumala]], the second-largest island, led by the Kuvari leader [[Mariba Kuna]]. The two administrations were generally unfriendly toward one another in the aftermath of the insurgency, but developed levels of rapprochement over time, and the division was superseded by the modern structure of fourteen districts, one for each major island. Stronger bonds eventually formed as the islands were cast aside by the other members of the League, whose Burawi and island communities treated the Bolodun and Kuvari islands with racism and contempt.

=== United Capuyaquiran Republic ===

The islands originally did not want to join the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]] in 1832, but did so out of fear of economic isolation, retaining close ties with Burawa, which remained deeply connected to the rest of the League.

=== Independence (1926) ===

The nation declared independence in 1926, after the [[Socialist Party of Bolodun]] (SPB) won a majority of seats in the islands' legislature. The SPB was backed by the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]] and built a multiracial coalition spanning the islands' communities. Because of the existing estrangement between the islands and the rest of the union, the secession was not met with conflict. During the [[Great Adonian War]], the islands were invaded by [[Volisania]].

== Politics ==

The Bolodun and Kuvari Islands are a multi-party socialist presidential republic. The government is similar to that of [[Burawa]]: a centralized state that manages and plans public services. The [[President of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|President]], currently [[Yohannes Bari]], is elected and heads the executive. Major parties include the [[Kadarist Party (Bolodun and Kuvari Islands)|Kadarist Party]], the [[Socialist Reform Party (Bolodun and Kuvari Islands)|Socialist Reform Party]], the [[Kuvari Peoples Indigenous Party]], and the [[New Kadarist Party]].

The legislature is the [[People's Assembly of Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|People's Assembly]], a unicameral body of 100 members elected from the country's fourteen districts. It was established in 1927 under the new constitution adopted after independence. The Assembly is the legislative branch of the state: it writes and passes laws, and exercises oversight of the government. Under the current distribution of seats, the Kadarist Party holds 65 seats, the Kuvari Peoples Indigenous Party 12, the Socialist Reform Party 11, the New Kadarist Party 8, and independents 4.

The state provides free public healthcare, education, and transportation to all citizens. A specialized university program sends willing students abroad for study, primarily to [[Balisca]] and [[Burawa]].

== Economy ==

The economy of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands has an estimated gross domestic product per capita of I$21,423, with a total GDP of approximately I$2.48 billion. The economy retains a significant agricultural base, a legacy of the sugar plantation era, with cash crops such as sugar a major export earner, alongside fishing, maritime services, and tourism. The islands have capitalized on their strong education system to become a popular destination for medical tourism, drawing patients from across the Capuyaquiran Sea and the wider region. Medical tourism and cash crop exports sustain the economy without reliance on foreign aid. The country has a [[Human Development Index]] of 0.951.

== Education ==

The Bolodun and Kuvari Islands report a literacy rate of 100 percent. Free public education is provided to all citizens. The state's university program sends willing students abroad to study, primarily in [[Balisca]] and [[Burawa]], a policy that reflects the country's small size and its close ties to the Burawi-speaking world.

== Demographics ==

The Bolodun and Kuvari Islands had an estimated population of 115,593 in 2025. The largest ethnic group is [[Sudanese Kaftians of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|Sudanese Kaftian]], making up 57.2 percent of the population, concentrated on Bolodun; the [[Kuvari people|Kuvari]] form 35.5 percent, concentrated on [[Tumala]] and the eastern islands; and [[Burawi people of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|Burawi]] make up 6.5 percent. The remaining 0.8 percent is classified as other.

